 ## Overview
-The NeuS training loss is a composite objective function used to optimize neural implicit surface representations (specifically Signed Distance Fields or Unsigned Distance Fields) from multi-view images without 3D ground-truth supervision [S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of recovering sharp, high-fidelity geometry by replacing the standard NeRF density field with an SDF/UDF and regularizing it via volume rendering constraints [S3, unofficial]. It differs from standard NeRF training by enforcing the Eikonal equation (gradient norm = 1) to ensure the field represents a true distance function, and by incorporating mask and sparsity losses to resolve the ambiguity between surface location and opacity in unobserved regions [S1].
+The NeuS training loss is a composite objective function used to optimize neural implicit surface representations (Signed Distance Fields or Unsigned Distance Fields) from multi-view images without 3D ground-truth supervision [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of recovering sharp, high-fidelity geometry by replacing the standard NeRF density field with an SDF/UDF and regularizing it via volume rendering constraints [S3, unofficial]. It differs from standard NeRF training by enforcing the Eikonal equation (gradient norm = 1) to ensure the field represents a true distance function, and by incorporating mask and sparsity losses to resolve the ambiguity between surface location and opacity in unobserved regions [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
